Hi Gang!

NetHack in German requires some non-ASCII characters: ÄÖÜäöüß.

Darwin seems to insist on having them in UTF-8. I can't seem to make it  
display them in ISO-8859-15 encoding.

Fortunately, GCC 4 and the iconv library are there, so the conversion  
itself is a trivial problem.

The question is, should I implement TTY output in UTF-8 encoding as a  
compile-time option, or as a new windowing port based on the regular TTY  
port, so that it's selectable at run-time?

If it's a compile-time option, I have to change a finite number of  
character constants in the program into string constants (and change  
comparison operators into strcmp calls) to allow for UTF-8's multibyte  
characters.

If it's a new port, then the iconv library gets linked into the program  
for on-the-fly conversion, creating either a dynamically-linked library  
dependency (not likely to be a problem on systems modern enough to even  
have a UTF-8 locale) or a big fat statically-linked executable.

Anyone have any reasons I should choose one solution or the other, i.e.  
the compile-time option or the new port?

Tony

